Author: Bruno Veiga <Bruno.VeigadeAlmeida@windriver.com>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 12:37:01 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] ts2phc, phc2sys: add PHC readiness gate to prevent 37s UTC
 offset error

After every T-GM reboot, phc2sys applies the UTC offset correction
(-O 37) while the PHC is still on UTC because ts2phc has not yet
locked the clock to TAI. This produces a 37-second error in
CLOCK_REALTIME that takes ~10 minutes to recover via slewing.

Add a readiness gate between ts2phc and phc2sys:

ts2phc readiness indicator:
- On first SERVO_JUMP or SERVO_LOCKED transition, ts2phc writes
  /run/ptp-ready/<phc_device>.ready containing timestamp and servo
  state for debugging. SERVO_JUMP qualifies because it means the
  servo has stepped the PHC to within its pull-in range — the
  PHC-to-TAI error is already small enough for phc2sys's own servo
  to converge on the residual offset without applying the full UTC
  correction prematurely.
- On startup, ts2phc removes its own stale readiness file to prevent
  false positives from a previous boot.

phc2sys startup gate:
- Before entering the sync loop, phc2sys performs a non-blocking
  check for /run/ptp-ready/<source_phc>.ready (derived from its -s
  source clock).
- On file detection: gate releases, UTC offset correction applied
  normally.
- If the file is absent: servo starts with UTC offset suppressed
  (-O 0) and logs a warning; the do_loop() poll re-checks each
  iteration until the readiness file appears, then restores
  the configured offset. Using indefinite polling with -O 0 is safe
  because:
    (a) it eliminates any race between an arbitrary timeout and
        actual GNSS lock time (which varies by antenna environment);
    (b) -O 0 is operationally harmless — phc2sys simply syncs
        CLOCK_REALTIME to the PHC without adding any UTC correction,
        meaning the offset between the two clocks is always kept
        minimal (no 37s error); and
    (c) on T-BC nodes where no ts2phc runs, phc2sys will operate
        with -O 0 indefinitely which is correct since the PHC is
        already disciplined via ptp4l/BMC and the UTC offset is
        applied through the PTP protocol path.

The readiness file path is derived by convention from the PHC device
each process already knows — no new user-facing configuration
parameters are introduced.

